  <!-- UserGuide: Page Header -->
  <data name="UserGuide_PageHeaderTitle.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>USER GUIDE</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PageHeaderDescription.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Everything you need to know to get the most out of Agent-X.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Welcome (Row 1) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_Welcome_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Welcome to Agent-X</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Welcome_Description1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Agent-X is your local-first AI personal intelligence hub — a complete operating system for thinking, researching, writing, and reasoning over your own knowledge. All processing happens on your machine by default, your documents are stored in an encrypted local SQLite vault, and your data never leaves your device unless you explicitly opt into cloud providers.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Welcome_Description2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Agent-X unifies a remarkable breadth of capabilities under one roof: chat with local Ollama models or cloud frontier models from OpenAI and Anthropic; ask grounded questions about your own documents using Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) with citations; build a curated Knowledge Vault with auto-tagging, duplicate detection, and a force-directed Knowledge Graph; run hybrid semantic+keyword search with FTS5 and Reciprocal Rank Fusion; capture web content via the browser extension or built-in scraper; transcribe audio; annotate PDFs; automate recurring work with Workflows, Scheduled Queries, and Automation Rules; track how your beliefs evolve over time with Temporal Identity; and extend the platform with plugins, calendar/email connectors, and a mobile companion that pairs over QR.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Welcome_Description3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>This guide is the definitive reference for every feature, surface, shortcut, and integration in Agent-X. Read it cover-to-cover for the full mental model, or jump to the section you need from the sidebar. The Command Palette (Ctrl+K) and Jump-To dialog (Ctrl+P) make every page reachable in two keystrokes from anywhere in the app.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Getting Started (Row 2) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingStarted_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Getting Started</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingStarted_Prerequisites_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Prerequisites</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingStarted_Prereq1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Install Ollama — Download and install Ollama from ollama.com (Windows, macOS, and Linux are supported). Ollama is the local AI runtime that powers on-device inference in Agent-X. Cloud providers (OpenAI, Anthropic) work without Ollama, but local-first mode requires it.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingStarted_Prereq2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Pull at least one chat model — Open a terminal and run</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingStarted_Prereq2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>llama3.2 is a strong default for general chat and works well on 8 GB VRAM systems. For RAG, document indexing, and Semantic Search, you also need an embedding model — the all-minilm model is small (~46 MB), fast, and produces 384-dim vectors that the vault is calibrated against:</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingStarted_Prereq3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Ensure Ollama is running — Ollama must be actively serving on http://localhost:11434 (the default) before launching Agent-X. Start the daemon with</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingStarted_FirstLaunch_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>First Launch &amp; Onboarding</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingStarted_FirstLaunch_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The first time you open Agent-X the Onboarding wizard takes over. It auto-detects your Ollama installation, runs a connectivity probe against the configured endpoint, lists every locally installed model so you can pick a default chat model and a default embedding model, runs the Hardware Advisor to surface model-size recommendations for your CPU/GPU/VRAM, and walks you through importing your first folder of documents into the Knowledge Vault.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingStarted_FirstLaunch_Tip.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>You can re-run the wizard at any time from the Dashboard's Setup AI tile, or jump straight to Settings &gt; AI Provider to reconfigure individual fields. Onboarding state is stored locally and never blocks subsequent launches.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Dashboard (Row 3) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Dashboard</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Dashboard is the command center of Agent-X — a single screen that surfaces system health, Knowledge Vault statistics, recent activity, identity insights, and the four most-used quick actions. Open it any time with Ctrl+D, or land on it automatically when you launch the app.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_Features_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>What you will find on the Dashboard:</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_Feature1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>System Status Cards — Live indicators showing Ollama connectivity (probed against your configured endpoint, default http://localhost:11434), your active chat and embedding models, total indexed documents, and total chunks.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_Feature2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Knowledge Vault Statistics — Total documents imported, total chunks indexed, vector dimensionality, on-disk storage footprint, indexing-queue depth, and last-import timestamp. A drill-in opens the Knowledge Vault filtered to the matching subset.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_Feature3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Recent Activity Timeline — A reverse-chronological feed of conversations, document imports, search queries, Quick Action runs, workflow executions, scheduled-query firings, and detected insights. Click any entry to jump straight back to that context.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_Feature4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>AI Connection &amp; Identity Health — A real-time health check on Ollama plus latency to the active model, surface counts of recently captured beliefs, insights, and unresolved temporal-identity conflicts so you can pull threads on what your system has learned about you this week.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_QuickActions_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Quick-Action Buttons</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_QuickActions_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Dashboard features four prominent quick-action buttons for the most common workflows:</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_QuickAction1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Setup AI</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_QuickAction1_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Re-run the onboarding wizard to reconfigure Ollama and models.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_QuickAction2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Import Documents</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_QuickAction2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Add files to your Knowledge Vault for AI-powered analysis.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_QuickAction3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Start Chat</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_QuickAction3_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open a new AI conversation with your selected model.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_QuickAction4_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Semantic Search</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Dashboard_QuickAction4_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Search your documents by meaning, not just keywords.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: AI Chat (Row 4) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>AI Chat</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>AI Chat is the conversational core of Agent-X. Hold natural-language conversations with any installed Ollama model running locally, or with frontier cloud models from OpenAI (GPT-4o, GPT-4o-mini, o1, o3) and Anthropic (Claude Sonnet 4, Opus, Haiku) when you opt in. Conversations stream token-by-token, render full Markdown including code blocks with syntax highlighting, and persist to a local SQLite database with full search.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Using_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Using AI Chat</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Step1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Select a model — Use the provider/model dropdown at the top of the chat to pick any installed Ollama model, or any configured cloud model. The dropdown groups models by provider, shows parameter size and quantization for local models, and indicates context-window length so you can match the model to the task.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Step2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Type your message — Enter your prompt in the composer at the bottom. Press Enter to send, Shift+Enter for a new line, or Ctrl+Enter to send while keeping the input focused. The composer supports Markdown shortcuts and pasting code or images (for vision-capable models).</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Step3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Watch the response stream — Tokens stream in real-time over the local Ollama HTTP API or the cloud provider's SSE stream. Markdown is rendered live including headings, lists, tables, blockquotes, fenced code with language-aware syntax highlighting, and inline LaTeX. Press Esc to stop generation; the partial response is preserved.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Conversation_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Conversation Management</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Conversation1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Conversations auto-save after every turn to the encrypted local SQLite vault. The sidebar groups conversations by date and provides full-text search across every message you have ever sent — no cloud sync required.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Conversation2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Press Ctrl+N to start a fresh conversation. Each conversation has its own context window, system prompt, and pinned model. Conversations also support branching: hover any assistant message and click "Branch from here" to fork the conversation and explore an alternate path without losing the original thread.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Conversation3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Pin important conversations to the top of the sidebar, rename them inline, archive them out of the active list, or delete individually. Bulk-delete and a "Clear all" action are available, and an export-then-delete flow lets you save a conversation before purging.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Advanced_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Advanced Chat Features</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Advanced1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Conversation Branching — Fork any assistant message into an alternate timeline to test different prompts, regenerate with a different model, or explore "what if" paths. The full branch tree is visualized in the conversation header so you can switch branches without losing the original thread, and any branch can be promoted to the main line. Conversation Export serializes the active branch to Markdown, plain text, or JSON.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Advanced2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>System Prompts &amp; Code Tooling — Define and reuse custom system prompts (personas, role briefs, output-format guides) from the System Prompts library, applied per-conversation or as a default. Code blocks render with language-aware syntax highlighting, one-click Copy and Save-to-File, side-by-side diffs for patches, and smart-collapse for long blocks.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Advanced3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Conversation Memory — Agent-X automatically extracts durable facts (people, projects, preferences, decisions, technical notes) from your conversations into a structured memory store, so the AI stays grounded across separate sessions without you re-explaining context. Memory is local-only and inspectable from the Past Self surface.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AiChat_Advanced4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Suggested Follow-Ups &amp; Cost Tracking — After every response, three contextual follow-up questions are generated to deepen the inquiry. When using cloud providers, per-message token usage and cost are calculated against the live rate card and shown inline; session and lifetime spend roll up in Settings &gt; Cost Tracking.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Ask Your Files (Row 5) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_AskYourFiles_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Ask Your Files (RAG)</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AskYourFiles_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) is the core intelligence feature of Agent-X. Ask a natural-language question, and the system retrieves the most semantically relevant passages from your own indexed documents, hands them to the chat model as grounded context, and produces an answer with inline numbered citations that link back to the exact source passage. Every claim is traceable; nothing is invented out of thin air.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AskYourFiles_HowItWorks_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>How It Works</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AskYourFiles_Step1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Select scope — Pick a single document, a multi-select of documents, or an entire Collection from the Knowledge Vault. Scoping narrows retrieval to the most relevant subset, dramatically improving precision when your vault grows large.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AskYourFiles_Step2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Ask a question — Type a natural-language question. Agent-X also supports HyDE (Hypothetical Document Embeddings) for harder queries: the model first drafts a plausible answer, then uses that draft's embedding to retrieve real passages — boosting recall for abstract questions.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AskYourFiles_Step3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Hybrid retrieval — Your query embedding is compared against the HNSW vector index using cosine similarity, while FTS5 runs a parallel keyword search. Results are fused via Reciprocal Rank Fusion (k=60) and the top-K passages (default 10, configurable) are selected, with optional MMR diversification to avoid near-duplicate chunks.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AskYourFiles_Step4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Grounded answer — Retrieved passages are injected into the chat model's prompt as labeled context blocks. The model is instructed to cite specific passages by number, and a final guard pass verifies every numbered citation maps to an actual retrieved chunk before rendering.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AskYourFiles_Citations_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Citation System</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AskYourFiles_Citations_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Every RAG answer ships with numbered citations [1], [2], [3]… that map directly to the retrieved chunks. Each citation chip shows the source document name, page number for PDFs (or chunk index for other formats), the relevance score, and a hover preview of the retrieved passage. Clicking any citation opens the source document at the exact location with the matched span highlighted, so you can verify, quote, and expand the surrounding context. RAG can also be invoked alongside Web Search — combined results show provenance for each source type so you always know what came from your vault versus the open web.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Quick Actions (Row 6) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Quick Actions</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Quick Actions are pre-built AI workflows that perform high-leverage document intelligence tasks with a single click. Select one or more documents (or entire Collections), choose an action, and Agent-X handles model selection, prompt engineering, chunk-aware processing for long documents, and structured output. Outputs can be inserted into a new conversation, exported, or saved as Annotations on the source document.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Available_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Available Actions</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Action1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Summarize</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Action1_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Generate a concise summary capturing themes, arguments, and conclusions. Choose length presets (one-line, paragraph, executive, detailed) and audience (general, technical, executive). Long documents use map-reduce summarization across chunks for coherent results without context-window truncation.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Action2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Extract Key Points</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Action2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Pull the most important facts, figures, arguments, and takeaways into structured bullet points. Output is grouped by section heading when the source has structure, and each bullet is anchored to its source chunk so you can drill back to context.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Action3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Translate</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Action3_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Translate document content into 30+ supported languages while preserving headings, lists, code blocks, and tables. Bilingual side-by-side mode is available for review. Translation runs on the active chat model — pick a stronger model for nuanced or domain-heavy text.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Action4_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Rewrite</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Action4_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Rewrite content in a different tone, style, or complexity level — simplify for non-experts, formalize for executives, condense for the inbox, or expand for a long-form post. Useful upstream of the Generative Identity "Draft As Me" feature for refining drafts to your voice.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Action5_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Explain</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Action5_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Get a clear, layered explanation of complex topics, technical jargon, or dense material — ELI5 mode, intermediate, or expert. The action grounds explanations in the document's own definitions when present, so you get domain-faithful interpretations rather than generic ones.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Action6_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Q&amp;A Generation</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Action6_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Automatically generate question-and-answer pairs covering every key concept — ideal for study guides, quizzes, knowledge checks, and onboarding documents. Difficulty (easy / medium / hard) and count are configurable. Generated Q&amp;A can be exported to Markdown or piped into a flashcard tool.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_QuickActions_Usage.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Navigate to Quick Actions (or invoke from the Knowledge Vault context menu), pick documents (or a Collection), select the action tab, tune options, and click Run. Streaming progress is shown for long jobs. Output renders in the results pane with copy, export-to-Markdown, save-as-Annotation, and "Continue in Chat" actions.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Knowledge Vault (Row 7) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Knowledge Vault</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Knowledge Vault is your centralized, encrypted document repository. Every file you import is stored locally, parsed by a format-specific extractor, chunked into semantic segments, embedded into a vector index (HNSW) and a parallel FTS5 keyword index, auto-tagged, deduplicated, and surfaced for search, RAG, Quick Actions, the Knowledge Graph, and Workflows. The vault is the source of truth that every other intelligence feature in Agent-X reads from.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Importing_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Importing Documents</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Importing_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>There are five ways to add documents to your Knowledge Vault:</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Import1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Drag and drop — Drag files or entire folders onto the Knowledge Vault page. Supported formats are imported automatically; unsupported formats are skipped with a clear log entry. Folder imports preserve relative paths as searchable metadata.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Import2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Import button — Use the Import button to open a multi-select system file picker, or paste URLs to ingest web content directly via the built-in scraper (Web Import). The Import flow batches submissions to the indexing queue so the UI stays responsive while large folders process.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Import3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Folder Monitor — Add a folder to the watch list (Settings &gt; Knowledge Vault) and Agent-X will auto-import any new file dropped into it via the FileWatcher service. Combined with cloud-sync folders (OneDrive, Dropbox, iCloud Drive), this turns any folder into a live ingest pipeline.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Managing_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Managing Documents</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Managing_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Each row in the Knowledge Vault surfaces file name, format, size, import timestamp, chunk count, indexing status, AI-generated tags, and source path. From the row context menu and side preview pane you can:</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Manage1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Preview the full extracted text, view per-chunk breakdown with token counts, inspect metadata (author, page count, language detected), and see which Collections the document belongs to.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Manage2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Re-index a document after switching embedding models, changing chunk size, or upgrading the parser. Re-index is idempotent — the old vectors are dropped atomically and replaced when the new run completes successfully.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Manage3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Delete one or many documents at a time. Deletion removes the original file (configurable), all chunks, all embeddings, all FTS5 entries, and all Collection memberships. Cascading deletes are transactional, so the vault never ends up in a partial state.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Advanced_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Advanced Features</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Advanced1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Auto-Tagging — AI automatically generates semantic tags when documents are imported, categorizing content by topic, domain, and key concepts without manual effort.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Advanced2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Duplicate Detection — Smart duplicate and near-duplicate detection during import using content hashing and cosine similarity, preventing redundant documents from cluttering your vault.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Advanced3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Batch Operations — Select multiple documents for bulk operations including delete, re-index, or tag assignment, saving time when managing large document collections.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Advanced4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Advanced Filtering — Filter documents by collection, date range, and sort by name, date, size, or file type to quickly locate specific files in your vault.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Advanced5.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Document Preview Panel — A side panel showing document details, tags, chunks, and metadata at a glance without navigating away from the document list.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeVault_Advanced6.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>AI-Generated Titles — Generate descriptive, meaningful titles for documents using AI analysis of their content, replacing generic file names with informative labels.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Collections (Row 8) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_Collections_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Collections</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Collections_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Collections are the organizational layer above the Knowledge Vault. Think of them as smart folders that any document can belong to multiple times — a single research paper can simultaneously live in "AI Safety," "2025 Reading," and "Cited in Q4 Report." Collections drive scoped retrieval: every Quick Action, Semantic Search, RAG query, Workflow, and Automation Rule can be scoped to a Collection so the AI only sees what is relevant to the task.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Collections_Working_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Working with Collections</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Collections_Working1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Create — From the Collections page click "New Collection," give it a descriptive name, an optional description, and pick a color tag. Collections are also auto-suggested by the Organization Suggestion service: when a cluster of recently imported documents shares strong topical overlap, Agent-X proposes grouping them and you confirm with one click.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Collections_Working2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Populate — Add documents via multi-select and "Add to Collection," via right-click in the Knowledge Vault, by dragging onto the Collection in the sidebar, or in bulk from a Workflow. A document can belong to any number of Collections — membership is a relationship, not an exclusive folder.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Collections_Working3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Scope retrieval — In Search, Ask Your Files, Workflows, Quick Actions, and Automation Rules, pick a Collection from the scope selector to confine retrieval to its members only. Combine multiple Collections with AND/OR logic for advanced queries like "Q4 docs AND tagged 'pricing'." Scoped retrieval typically improves answer precision by 20-40% on focused tasks.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Collections_Working4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Manage &amp; share — Rename inline, edit descriptions, reorder via drag-and-drop, archive Collections you no longer use, or delete (deleting a Collection unbinds the documents but never removes them from the vault). Collections are first-class in Backup &amp; Restore, Export, and the Knowledge Graph, where each Collection appears as a hub node connecting all its members.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Knowledge Graph (Row 9) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeGraph_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Knowledge Graph</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeGraph_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Knowledge Graph turns your Knowledge Vault into an interactive, force-directed network visualization. Documents, Collections, and tags become nodes; collection memberships and topical similarities become edges. Open it from the sidebar (Ctrl+G) to see clusters of related work emerge organically, surface unexpected connections between projects, and navigate your vault visually instead of through file lists.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeGraph_Elements_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Graph Elements</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeGraph_Element1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Document Nodes — Blue circles sized proportionally to chunk count, so high-content documents draw the eye. Hovering surfaces title, format, and primary tags; the side preview pane shows a passage excerpt without leaving the graph.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeGraph_Element2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Collection Nodes — Purple hub nodes that visually anchor all their member documents. The diameter of a Collection node scales with member count, making heavy Collections instantly identifiable across a large vault.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeGraph_Element3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Tag Nodes — Amber nodes that surface auto-generated semantic tags. Tag nodes act as topical bridges: two unrelated Collections that share a tag will pull together in the layout, revealing thematic overlap that isn't obvious from folder structure alone.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeGraph_Element4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Edges — Solid lines mark Collection membership; dashed lines mark tag associations and high-similarity links between documents (cosine similarity above the configured threshold). Edge weight is encoded in line thickness so dense clusters and weak peripheral links are easy to distinguish at a glance.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeGraph_Interaction_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Interaction</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeGraph_Interaction1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Click to Navigate — Click any node to navigate directly to that document, Collection, or tag-filtered view, seamlessly bridging visual exploration and detailed content access. Right-click opens contextual actions: pin, isolate, expand neighbors, or run a Quick Action on the node.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeGraph_Interaction2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Zoom, Pan, and Filter — Mouse wheel to zoom, click-drag to pan, double-click empty space to reset, and use the filter chips at the top to hide tag nodes, dim Collections, or constrain to a date range. Search the graph by name and the matched node is highlighted and centered.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KnowledgeGraph_Interaction3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Physics &amp; Layouts — A force-directed simulation pulls related nodes together and pushes unrelated nodes apart. Switch between force-directed, radial, and hierarchical layouts to suit the question at hand. Pause physics to lock the layout for screenshots, or step through clusters using the navigator panel for large graphs.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Weekly Digest (Row 10) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_WeeklyDigest_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Weekly Digest</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WeeklyDigest_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Weekly Digest is your AI-generated activity report — a high-signal recap of what you read, asked, decided, and discovered over a configurable window. It synthesizes activity across the Knowledge Vault, AI Chat, Search, Workflows, and Insight Harvesting into a single narrative document so you can see your week of thinking at a glance, share progress with collaborators, or feed it back into your own planning rituals.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WeeklyDigest_Features_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Digest Features</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WeeklyDigest_Feature1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>AI-Generated Narrative — Instead of a raw activity log, the digest is a coherent prose report grouped into themes: documents imported, conversations held, key questions asked, insights captured, beliefs that shifted, and Workflows that ran. The model is prompted to highlight what matters and skip noise.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WeeklyDigest_Feature2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Activity Summary &amp; Citations — Every claim in the digest links back to its source: a specific conversation, a Knowledge Vault document, an insight card, or a Workflow run. Click any citation to drill into the underlying context. Unlike a generic dashboard, the digest is verifiable.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WeeklyDigest_Feature3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Configurable Cadence — Pick daily, weekly (the default), bi-weekly, or monthly. Each digest run is a separate timestamped document in the Knowledge Vault, so the digests themselves become a longitudinal record you can search across. Combine with Scheduled Queries to deliver digests automatically every Sunday at 6 PM.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WeeklyDigest_Feature4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Export &amp; Share — One-click export to Markdown, PDF, or plain text. Adjustable templates control sections, depth, and audience tone (personal log vs. executive summary vs. technical retrospective). Optional automatic email delivery if Email Integration is configured.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WeeklyDigest_Feature5.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Automatic Insight Surfacing — The digest engine flags trends (rising topics, declining engagement), highlights documents you imported but never queried, surfaces unresolved Temporal Identity conflicts, and proposes specific knowledge gaps to fill — turning the digest from a review into a forward-looking action list.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Semantic Search (Row 11) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_SemanticSearch_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Semantic Search</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_SemanticSearch_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Semantic Search transcends keyword matching by understanding the meaning of your query. Ask "how does our login flow handle expired sessions?" and Agent-X surfaces passages discussing token refresh, session timeouts, and re-authentication — even if those passages never use the word "expired" or "login." Combined with FTS5 keyword search and Reciprocal Rank Fusion in Hybrid mode, it gives you the best of vector recall and lexical precision in one ranked list.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_SemanticSearch_HowItWorks_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>How Vector Search Works</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_SemanticSearch_HowItWorks_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>At import time, every document is split into overlapping chunks (default 512 tokens, 64-token overlap, configurable in Settings). Each chunk is converted to a high-dimensional vector by your selected embedding model — for all-minilm that's 384 dimensions, for nomic-embed-text or Ollama's mxbai-embed-large it's 768 or 1024. Vectors are stored in an HNSW index for O(log n) approximate nearest-neighbor lookup. At query time your question is embedded with the same model, cosine similarity is computed against the index, and the top-K matches return with similarity scores, source pointers, and chunk previews.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_SemanticSearch_Using_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Using Semantic Search</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_SemanticSearch_Using1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Natural-language queries — Type a question, a topic, or a partial phrase: "how does the authentication system work?", "Q3 revenue figures with regional breakdowns," "passages where I argued against monorepos." The model interprets intent, not just keywords.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_SemanticSearch_Using2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Similarity scoring — Each result shows a relevance score from 0 (unrelated) to 1 (identical match). Hybrid mode shows the fused score plus the underlying semantic and keyword sub-scores, so you can see exactly why a result ranked where it did.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_SemanticSearch_Using3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Filtering &amp; scoping — Constrain by Collection, tag, file type, date range, language, or source path. Filters compose with AND/OR logic and persist across queries within a session, so you can iterate on a research thread without re-applying the scope each time.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_SemanticSearch_Using4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Result previews — Each result renders the matched passage with the most relevant span highlighted, source document name, page or chunk index, surrounding sentences for context, and one-click actions: Open in Vault, Cite in Chat, Add to a Quick Action, or save as an Annotation.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_SemanticSearch_Using5.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Three search modes — Semantic (pure vector similarity, best for conceptual queries), Keyword (FTS5 full-text, best for exact terms, names, IDs, and code), and Hybrid (Reciprocal Rank Fusion with k=60, best for general use). Switch modes in the search bar; Hybrid is the default and recommended starting point.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_SemanticSearch_Using6.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Search History &amp; Cache — Every query is saved to a searchable history that persists across sessions, with re-run, pin, and delete actions. Frequent queries are served from the SearchCache for sub-second responses; the cache invalidates automatically when documents are added, removed, or re-indexed so results never go stale.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Model Manager (Row 12) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_ModelManager_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Model Manager</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_ModelManager_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Model Manager is your visual control panel for every model Agent-X can use — local Ollama models you have installed, cloud models you have configured (OpenAI, Anthropic), plus a curated catalog of recommended Ollama models you can pull with a click. Browse, inspect, benchmark, pull, delete, set defaults, and assign per-task models without ever touching the command line.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_ModelManager_Capabilities_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Capabilities</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_ModelManager_Capability1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>View installed models — Every Ollama model on your system listed with name, size on disk, parameter count, quantization (Q4_0, Q4_K_M, Q5_K_M, Q8_0, FP16), context-window length, and last-used timestamp. Sort and filter to find what you need fast.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_ModelManager_Capability2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Model details &amp; benchmarking — Click any model for full details (family, format, license, system prompt template, modelfile contents) and a one-click "Benchmark this model" action that measures load time, first-token latency, sustained tokens-per-second, and memory residency on your hardware. Results are pinned to the model card.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_ModelManager_Capability3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Pull new models — Type a model name (mistral, codellama, phi3, qwen2.5, gemma3, etc.) or pick from the curated catalog and click Pull. A real-time progress bar shows download speed and ETA; pull is resumable if your connection drops mid-download.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_ModelManager_Capability4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Delete models — Reclaim disk by removing models you no longer use. Multi-select supports bulk delete. Models currently in use by an active conversation, Workflow, or scheduled query are protected from accidental deletion until you explicitly confirm.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_ModelManager_Capability5.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Set default and per-task models — Assign system-wide default chat and embedding models, plus per-task overrides for Quick Actions, Workflows, RAG, and Web Search. The Model Manager also surfaces Hardware Advisor compatibility scoring so you can immediately see which models are well-matched to your hardware.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Hardware Advisor (Row 13) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_HardwareAdvisor_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Hardware Advisor</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_HardwareAdvisor_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Hardware Advisor profiles your machine and translates it into concrete model recommendations. Run it once during onboarding (it runs automatically) or any time you upgrade your CPU, GPU, or RAM. The advisor enumerates compute devices, measures real available VRAM and system RAM headroom, benchmarks token-per-second throughput against your installed models, and turns the numbers into a ranked list of models that will run well on your specific hardware — including suggested quantization, context length, and GPU offload settings.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_HardwareAdvisor_Detects_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>What It Detects</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_HardwareAdvisor_Detect1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>GPU detection — Enumerates every discrete and integrated GPU via WMI/DirectX. NVIDIA cards report driver version, CUDA capability, and Compute Capability. AMD cards report ROCm support status. Intel Arc reports oneAPI/OpenVINO availability. The advisor flags configurations where Ollama can hardware-accelerate vs CPU-only.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_HardwareAdvisor_Detect2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>VRAM &amp; system memory analysis — Reports total and currently free VRAM, distinguishes between dedicated and shared memory on integrated GPUs, and surfaces system RAM headroom. The advisor accounts for OS overhead and other running apps so the recommendation reflects real available capacity, not theoretical maximum.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_HardwareAdvisor_Detect3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Recommended model sizes — Maps your VRAM to model classes: under 4 GB → 1B-3B with Q4 quantization; 6-8 GB → 7B with Q4_K_M; 10-12 GB → 13B with Q5_K_M; 16+ GB → 34B with Q4 or 7B at FP16; 24+ GB → 70B with Q3_K_S. Embedding models (all-minilm, nomic-embed-text, mxbai-embed-large) are matched to embedding-friendly hardware separately.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_HardwareAdvisor_Detect4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Performance tuning &amp; warnings — Actionable recommendations: pull a quantized variant (Q4_K_M is usually the sweet spot), reduce context length if VRAM is tight, set num_gpu layers to maximize GPU offload without spilling to CPU, enable flash attention if supported, and warnings when the active model is fundamentally too large for the device — including the projected slowdown if you proceed anyway.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Settings (Row 14) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_Settings_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Settings</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Settings_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Settings is your full configuration surface. Every preference is persisted to a local JSON file, encrypted-at-rest where it contains secrets, and exported as part of Backup &amp; Restore. The page is organized into four collapsible groups — AI Provider, Inference, Knowledge Vault, and Cost Tracking — plus deep links to specialized surfaces like Sync, Encryption, Workspace Profiles, and Feature Flags.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Settings_Group1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>AI Provider</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Settings_Group1_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Configure every model provider: Ollama endpoint URL (default http://localhost:11434, override for remote Ollama hosts); default chat and embedding models; OpenAI API key, organization, and active model (GPT-4o, GPT-4o-mini, o1, o3); Anthropic API key and active Claude model (Sonnet 4, Opus, Haiku); per-provider request timeouts, retry policies, and concurrent-request caps. Keys are stored DPAPI-encrypted on disk.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Settings_Group2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Inference</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Settings_Group2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Tune generation parameters: temperature (0.0 deterministic → 1.0 creative), top_p, top_k, frequency/presence penalties, max_tokens per response, default context window size, and stop sequences. Per-conversation overrides exist in Chat — these are the system-wide defaults for new conversations.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Settings_Group3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Knowledge Vault</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Settings_Group3_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Control indexing behavior: chunk size (default 512 tokens), chunk overlap (default 64), retrieval top-K (default 10), HNSW M and efSearch parameters, MMR diversification toggle and lambda, search mode default (Hybrid / Semantic / Keyword), folder-monitor watch list, auto-tagging toggle, duplicate-detection sensitivity, and JS rendering policy for Web Import.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Settings_Group4_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Cost Tracking</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Settings_Group4_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>For cloud providers, track per-message token usage and dollar cost against the live OpenAI and Anthropic rate cards. Drill into per-session, daily, weekly, and lifetime spend; break down by model, conversation, and feature (Chat, RAG, Quick Action, Workflow). Set monthly soft and hard budget caps that pause cloud calls automatically when exceeded.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Settings_Shortcut.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Press Ctrl+, from anywhere in the app to jump to Settings. Use the search bar at the top of Settings to find any specific option in seconds.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Keyboard Shortcuts (Row 15) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Keyboard Shortcuts</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Agent-X is built for keyboard-first workflows. Every page is reachable in two keystrokes via the Command Palette (Ctrl+K) or Jump-To dialog (Ctrl+P), and the most common actions have dedicated chords. Press F1 at any time to open the live Cheatsheet — the shortcuts below mirror the global registry.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Table_Shortcut.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>SHORTCUT</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Table_Action.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>ACTION</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Command Palette — instantly find any page or action</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>New Conversation in AI Chat</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Knowledge Vault</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Semantic Search</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action5.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Semantic Search (alternate)</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action6.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Settings</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action7.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Close Command Palette / dialog / dropdown</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action8.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Show Keyboard Cheatsheet (live, searchable)</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action9.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Jump To — quick file/page navigator</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action10.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Dashboard</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action11.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Knowledge Graph</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action12.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Workflows</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action13.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Web Import</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action14.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Analytics</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action15.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Operations</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Action16.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Quick-jump to page 1-9 in the navigation order (Dashboard, Chat, AskFiles, Search, Vault, Collections, Workflows, Models, Settings)</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_KeyboardShortcuts_Footer.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Tip: All shortcuts are remappable. Open Settings &gt; Keyboard Shortcuts to rebind any chord, register multi-key sequences, or scope shortcuts to a specific page.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Command Palette (Row 16) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_CommandPalette_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Command Palette</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_CommandPalette_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Command Palette is the central nervous system for keyboard-driven navigation. Inspired by VS Code, Sublime, and Spotlight, it gives you fuzzy-matched, instant access to every page, every saved conversation, every Quick Action, every Workflow, every Collection, every settings panel, and every plugin command — through a single search box. Most experienced Agent-X users open the palette as their primary navigation surface and rarely touch the sidebar.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_CommandPalette_HowToUse_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>How to Use</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_CommandPalette_Step1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Press Ctrl+K (or Ctrl+Shift+P) from anywhere in the app to summon the palette overlay. The palette appears centered over the current page and captures input immediately.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_CommandPalette_Step2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Type to fuzzy-match — characters need not be contiguous, so "kbv" finds "Knowledge Vault" and "newchat" finds "New Conversation." Results group by category (Pages, Actions, Conversations, Documents, Workflows, Plugins) and rank by recency and frequency.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_CommandPalette_Step3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Use ↑/↓ to move through results, Enter to execute, Tab to peek into a result without leaving the palette, and Esc to dismiss. Prefix queries with ":" for actions ("`:export this conversation`"), "&gt;" for jump-to-document, or "?" to surface help articles directly from this user guide.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_CommandPalette_Coverage.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The palette indexes every navigable surface (Dashboard, Chat, Knowledge Vault, Search, Collections, Quick Actions, Workflows, Workspace Profiles, Annotations, Inbox, Operations, Analytics, Knowledge Graph, Plugins, Calendar, Email, Backup &amp; Restore, Settings, Model Manager, Hardware Advisor, this User Guide), every saved conversation by title, every recently opened document, and every plugin-contributed command. New surfaces register themselves automatically — no manual config needed.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Supported File Formats (Row 17) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Supported File Formats</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Agent-X ships with format-specific parsers tuned for the realities of each file type — selectable text from PDFs but OCR fallback when text is missing, structure-aware chunking for Markdown headings and code blocks, table extraction from Word, transcripts from YouTube URLs, and so on. Files that aren't supported natively can often be imported as plain text. Configure parser preferences and OCR language packs in Settings &gt; Knowledge Vault.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Table_Format.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>FORMAT</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Table_Extension.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>EXTENSION</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Table_Extraction.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>EXTRACTION METHOD</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_PDF.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>PDF</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_PDF_Extraction.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Selectable-text extraction with per-page tracking; falls back to OCR (Tesseract) for scanned/image-only PDFs. Tables, footnotes, and headings are preserved.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Word.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Word Document</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Word_Extraction.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Paragraph, list, and table extraction with heading hierarchy preserved. Tracked changes and comments are captured as metadata for surfacing in answers.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Text.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Plain Text</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Text_Extraction.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Direct ingestion with line-break-aware chunking; UTF-8/16/32 and common Windows code pages auto-detected.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Markdown.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Markdown</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Markdown_Extraction.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Parsed with heading-aware chunking, fenced code blocks kept intact, and front-matter (YAML/TOML) captured as searchable metadata.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Code.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Code Files</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Code_Extraction.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Syntax-aware chunking that respects function, class, namespace, and block boundaries via tree-sitter; comments and docstrings are weighted appropriately.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Images.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Images</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_Images_Extraction.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Tesseract OCR with auto language detection (English/Spanish/French/German/Chinese/Japanese by default; add packs in Settings). For vision-capable models, the image itself is also retained for direct VLM querying.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FileFormats_CodeList.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Code support spans .cs, .py, .js, .ts, .tsx, .jsx, .cpp, .c, .h, .hpp, .java, .go, .rs, .rb, .php, .swift, .kt, .scala, .clj, .ex, .erl, .lua, .r, .jl, .dart, .sh, .ps1, .yaml, .toml, .json, .xml, .sql, .css, .scss, .html, .vue, .svelte, and more. Plus: .epub, .rtf, .csv, .tsv, .xlsx (sheet/table extraction), .pptx (slide-text extraction), .html (readability extractor), audio (.mp3/.m4a/.wav via Audio Transcription), and YouTube URLs (transcript extraction).</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Troubleshooting (Row 18) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_Troubleshooting_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Troubleshooting</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Troubleshooting_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The most-reported issues and proven fixes. If something is broken, start here — most issues resolve in under five minutes. The Operations page (Ctrl+Shift+O) shows live diagnostics including model latency, indexing queue depth, error log tail, and last network attempt — useful for diagnosing anything not covered below.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Troubleshooting_Issue1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>"Ollama not detected"</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Troubleshooting_Issue1_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Confirm Ollama is running. Open a terminal and run "ollama serve" — if it errors with "address already in use," Ollama is already running, so the issue is endpoint config. Verify Settings &gt; AI Provider &gt; Ollama Endpoint matches the host Ollama is bound to (default http://localhost:11434). For remote Ollama, ensure the firewall allows the port and OLLAMA_HOST is set on the server. Click "Test Connection" in Settings to surface the exact failure mode.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Troubleshooting_Issue2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Slow responses</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Troubleshooting_Issue2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Run the Hardware Advisor — it benchmarks your active model and recommends a better-fitting variant when applicable. Quick fixes: switch from FP16 to Q4_K_M (≈50% VRAM savings, 2-3% quality drop); reduce context length to 4K for short Q&amp;A; ensure GPU offload is maximized (Settings &gt; Inference &gt; GPU Layers); close other heavy GPU consumers (Chrome, video editors) while running large models. If even small models lag, you may be CPU-only — confirm in Hardware Advisor and pick a model under 3B for usable speeds.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Troubleshooting_Issue3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Documents not indexing</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Troubleshooting_Issue3_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Three common causes: (1) the file format is not supported — see Supported File Formats; (2) no embedding model is configured — pull one with "ollama pull all-minilm" and select it in Settings &gt; AI Provider &gt; Embedding Model; (3) the indexing queue is paused or backed up — open Operations to see queue state and any errors. If a recent embedding-model swap left orphan vectors, use the Knowledge Vault context-menu "Re-index" action; bulk re-index is in Settings &gt; Knowledge Vault &gt; Maintenance.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Troubleshooting_Issue4_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>High memory usage</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Troubleshooting_Issue4_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Lower context window in Settings &gt; Inference (4K is plenty for most chat). Switch to a quantized model variant (Q4_K_M is the standard sweet spot). Cap the in-memory chunk window in Settings &gt; Knowledge Vault. Disable JS rendering for Web Import unless you specifically need it (each headless Chromium consumes ~200 MB). Close other memory-intensive apps. If the Knowledge Vault is huge (millions of chunks), enable on-disk HNSW with mmap to bound RAM usage at the cost of slightly slower retrieval.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Troubleshooting_Issue5_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Search returns no results</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Troubleshooting_Issue5_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Confirm indexing is complete (Knowledge Vault status column should be "Indexed," not "Queued" or "Failed"). Switch from Semantic to Hybrid mode — keyword search catches exact-term queries that vectors miss. Rephrase the question more descriptively for semantic search (vectors reward natural language, not single keywords). If you scoped to a Collection, broaden the scope. As a last resort, lower the relevance threshold in Settings &gt; Knowledge Vault &gt; Minimum Similarity to surface borderline matches you can manually triage.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Privacy & Security (Row 19) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_Privacy_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Privacy &amp; Security</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Privacy_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Privacy is not a feature in Agent-X — it is the architecture. Every design decision is biased toward keeping your data on your machine, in your control, with explicit and revocable consent for any external processing. The defaults are private; making it less private requires conscious opt-in.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Privacy_Point1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>All data stored locally — Documents, embeddings, conversations, insights, beliefs, voice profile, settings, plugin data, and metadata are stored in a local SQLite database (default path %LOCALAPPDATA%\Agent-X). Database encryption (SQLCipher / AES-256) is available to everyone and managed automatically via your Windows account; it is not recoverable by Strategia.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Privacy_Point2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Local-first by default — Every inference defaults to local Ollama. Cloud providers (OpenAI, Anthropic) are off until you explicitly enable them in Settings &gt; AI Provider, and even then per-conversation provider selection is one click. Web Search, Web Import, and any feature that touches the network is individually toggleable.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Privacy_Point3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>No telemetry — Agent-X collects no usage analytics, no crash reports, no model-call logs, no opt-out telemetry of any kind. The app makes zero outbound connections at startup beyond the Ollama probe to your configured local endpoint. If you want to verify, the network audit in Settings &gt; Privacy &amp; Security shows every domain Agent-X has ever connected to from this install.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Privacy_Point4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Your data, your control — Nothing is shared, uploaded, or used for training. The full Knowledge Vault, conversation history, and settings can be exported (Settings &gt; Backup) or wiped (Settings &gt; Privacy &amp; Security &gt; Reset All Data) on demand. Per-document and per-conversation deletion are immediate and cascading; deletion is final once committed.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Privacy_Point5.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Cloud-provider transparency — When a cloud provider is active, the active model badge in Chat is colored to indicate the provider, every message header records the provider used, and Cost Tracking logs the call. Cloud provider data handling is governed by each provider's policy: OpenAI does not train on API data by default; Anthropic does not train on API data; both retain logs for limited windows for abuse monitoring (refer to their current terms).</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Getting Help (Row 20) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingHelp_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Getting Help</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingHelp_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>If this guide does not answer your question, here are the fastest ways to reach a human or find an answer. Most issues — connection problems, slow performance, "where is feature X" — are covered in Troubleshooting and the in-app Cheatsheet (F1). For everything else, start with email, and we will route to the right specialist.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingHelp_Email_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Email Support</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingHelp_Email_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Bug reports, feature requests, or general questions. Include your Agent-X version (Settings &gt; About) and a reproduction step or screenshot — that's usually enough to diagnose 90% of issues on the first reply.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingHelp_Docs_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Documentation</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingHelp_Docs_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The online documentation portal hosts the full reference: deep-dive articles, video walkthroughs, the plugin developer SDK, REST API docs for the local API host, the mobile pairing protocol, and an exhaustive changelog. Every topic in this in-app guide has a longer-form companion article online with examples and architectural diagrams.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingHelp_Footer1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Agent-X by Strategia</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GettingHelp_Footer2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Local-first AI for everyone</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Temporal Identity (Row 11) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_TemporalIdentity_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Temporal Identity Coherence</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_TemporalIdentity_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Your thoughts and beliefs evolve over time — and most of that evolution is invisible to you until something forces a comparison. Temporal Identity Coherence is Agent-X's quiet observer: it reads every conversation, identifies belief-bearing statements, captures voice patterns, harvests insights, and detects when something you say today contradicts something you said three months ago. The result is a longitudinal map of your intellectual journey that you can browse, query, and learn from.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_TemporalIdentity_Tracking_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>What It Tracks</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_TemporalIdentity_Tracking1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Beliefs — Persistent opinions, positions, and convictions you express across conversations. Beliefs are extracted automatically from declarative statements ("I think X," "X is true," "we should X"), stored with timestamp and conversation context, and clustered into longitudinal threads so the same belief expressed five times is one tracked entity, not five.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_TemporalIdentity_Tracking2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Insights — Breakthrough moments and novel framings the system detects automatically (see the Insight Harvesting section). Insights are distinct from beliefs: an insight is a synthesis or realization at a moment in time; a belief is a position you hold over time.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_TemporalIdentity_Tracking3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Voice Patterns — Your distinctive writing style, sentence structures, vocabulary preferences, idiosyncratic phrases, and tonal range. The voice profile feeds Generative Identity ("Draft As Me") and updates incrementally with every new message you send.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_TemporalIdentity_Tracking4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Temporal Changes — How your thinking shifts across weeks and months. The system surfaces drift gently: when an old belief weakens, when a new theme rises, when your voice trends more formal, more technical, or more uncertain than its baseline.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_TemporalIdentity_BeliefEvolution_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Belief Evolution Timeline</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_TemporalIdentity_BeliefEvolution_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Past Self page renders your tracked beliefs as a timeline. Each row is a belief; each dot on its row is a moment that belief was expressed. Hover any dot to see the source conversation and surrounding context. Beliefs that strengthened, weakened, or pivoted are highlighted with directional indicators so you can immediately see which threads are evolving.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_TemporalIdentity_Conflicts_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Conflict Detection</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_TemporalIdentity_Conflicts_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>When a new statement contradicts an existing tracked belief above a configurable confidence threshold, Agent-X opens a conflict card. Conflict cards surface on the Dashboard and persist in Past Self until you mark them resolved (with one of: "I've changed my mind," "context-dependent," "I was wrong before," or "no real conflict"). Every resolution updates the model's understanding so it stops re-flagging the same shift.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_TemporalIdentity_Conflicts1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Conflicts are opportunities for reflection — they mark moments of growth, learning, or shifts in perspective. Most users discover that 60-80% of detected conflicts are genuine evolutions rather than inconsistencies, and the act of resolving them is itself valuable.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_TemporalIdentity_Conflicts2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Each conflict card shows your past belief verbatim with its source conversation, your new position with its source, the time delta, and the model's analysis of the contrast. You can resolve, dismiss, or annotate the conflict — annotations feed back into the longitudinal record so future you can see how past you reasoned about the change.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Past Self (Row 12) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Past Self</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Past Self is a time machine for your ideas. Slice your conversation history by date range, by topic, or by belief, and query that slice as if it were a separate person — your earlier self, with their context, vocabulary, and unfinished thinking. It is the most directly useful application of Temporal Identity: not just "what did I write?" but "what was I trying to figure out, and how did I get here?"</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_Timeline_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Timeline Navigation</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_Timeline_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Past Self page renders your full conversational history as a horizontal timeline with density bands per topic cluster. Drag a window to scope, double-click a band to drill into it, or use the date picker for precise ranges. Themes that dominated a period (work, research, personal) appear as colored stripes so you can find "that month I was deep in X" without remembering exact dates.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_Timeline1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Drag-select any window on the timeline to constrain the query scope. Common presets exist for last 7 days, last 30 days, last quarter, last year, since a named milestone, or "before I started using model X."</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_Timeline2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Scroll through months at a glance to see the ebb and flow of your focus — heavy bands mark intensive thinking periods, gaps mark fallow stretches, and tag overlays show topical drift. The timeline supports zoom from yearly down to hourly granularity.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_Timeline3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Compare beliefs across time by selecting two non-contiguous windows: Past Self renders a side-by-side comparison of your stated positions, voice patterns, and topical mix between the two periods.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_Querying_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Querying Past Self</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_Querying_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Ask natural-language questions scoped to the selected time window. The retrieval engine constrains the corpus to conversations within scope and prompts the model with a temporal anchor, so answers reflect what you knew, believed, and were exploring then — not your current synthesis. Useful queries: "What did I say about X?", "Which decisions did I make about X?", "What questions was I trying to answer?", "What was I uncertain about?"</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_QueryExample_Label.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Example query:</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_UseCases_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Use Cases</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_UseCase1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Intellectual Archaeology</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_UseCase1_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Rediscover forgotten ideas and trace the origin of current beliefs back to the conversation where they first appeared. "Where did I start believing X?" is one of the most common Past Self queries.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_UseCase2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Decision Review</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_UseCase2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Reconstruct the reasoning behind past decisions by reading your thoughts from that time. Useful for postmortems, strategic reviews, and learning from your own track record without the rationalization that hindsight introduces.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_UseCase3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Consistency Check</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_UseCase3_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Verify alignment between current actions and prior commitments. "Am I still on the path I committed to in Q1?" surfaces every prior statement that bears on the question, so you can spot drift before it compounds.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_UseCase4_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Growth Tracking</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PastSelf_UseCase4_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>See how your thinking has matured. The system can summarize the delta between two windows: vocabulary that entered or exited your active set, beliefs that resolved or pivoted, recurring questions that quietly disappeared because you answered them.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Generative Identity / Draft As Me (Row 13) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Generative Identity: Draft As Me</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>"Draft As Me" generates first drafts in your voice. The model is conditioned on a continuously updated voice profile assembled from your own messages — sentence patterns, vocabulary, tone, idioms, and idiosyncratic phrases — so the output reads like something you would have written, not like generic LLM prose. Use it for first drafts of emails, posts, memos, blog drafts, talk outlines, journal entries — any time the activation energy of starting is the bottleneck.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_HowItWorks_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>How It Works</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_Step1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Agent-X continuously analyzes your messages and conversation history to build a multi-dimensional voice profile: sentence-length distribution, parts-of-speech tendencies, lexical fingerprint, characteristic transitions, formality range, and signature phrases. The profile updates every time you send a new message — your voice grows with you.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_Step2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>When you request a draft, the system retrieves the relevant voice slice (you can pick "current voice," "past-self voice from window X," or a specific tone register), translates it into a structured style brief, and prompts the model to generate within those constraints.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_Step3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The generated draft appears inline in the chat with a "feels like me / does not feel like me" rating prompt. Ratings flow back into the voice profile to refine future generations. Edit, regenerate with adjusted parameters (more concise, more technical, longer), or accept and ship.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_Voice_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Voice Analysis</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_Voice_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Your voice profile is built from patterns extracted from your messages over time:</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_Voice1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Sentence patterns — Average length, distribution of short/medium/long sentences, complex vs. simple structures, punctuation habits, opening tendencies, and rhythm. The model attempts to mirror your cadence rather than smoothing it into AI-default prose.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_Voice2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Vocabulary &amp; signature phrases — High-frequency words and phrases that mark your writing, domain-specific terminology, characteristic transitions, and lexical "tells" that distinguish your voice from your peers'. Generic AI-isms are explicitly suppressed.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_Voice3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Tone and formality range — Whether you tend casual, executive, technical, exploratory, or contrarian. The profile holds multiple registers, so you can request "Draft As Me — formal" for a board memo or "Draft As Me — casual" for a Slack reply, and each pulls the right slice.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_Access_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Accessing Draft As Me</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_Access_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Draft As Me lives in the Past Self page and as a slash-command in any conversation:</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_Access1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>From Past Self, open the "Draft As Me" panel and enter your topic, target length, and tone register. The optional "anchor period" lets you draft in your voice from a specific window in the past — useful for matching the tone of an old project.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_GenerativeIdentity_Access2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>From any chat, type "/draft" followed by your topic and the model generates a draft in-line in your active voice profile. Iterate with "/refine more concise" or "/refine more technical" to shape the output.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Insight Harvesting (Row 14) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Insight Harvesting</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Most insights happen mid-conversation, evaporate within minutes, and are never recovered. Insight Harvesting closes that leak. After every conversation, the system scans the transcript for breakthrough moments, novel connections, and synthesis events, and preserves them as durable insight cards. The result is a personal "best of" archive that surfaces on the Dashboard, threads into your Weekly Digest, and becomes searchable from anywhere in the app.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_Automatic_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Automatic Detection</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_Automatic_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The detector runs on every closed conversation and looks for several signals:</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_Automatic1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Novel connections — Statements that bridge two previously unrelated concepts in your conversation history. The system tracks your topic graph and flags moments when an edge appears between two subgraphs that had no prior link.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_Automatic2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Realization language — Surface markers like "I just realized," "the key insight is," "that changes how I think about X," "wait — actually," and "this is the core point." The lexical signal alone is high-precision; combined with novelty scoring it becomes very high-precision.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_Automatic3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Synthesis moments — When the conversation compresses a multi-turn back-and-forth into a single crisp statement of understanding. These often happen at the end of a long thread and are the most valuable to preserve.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_Cards_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Insight Cards</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_Cards_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Each detected insight becomes a structured card containing:</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_CardExample_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>"Productivity is not about doing more — it is about doing less of what does not matter."</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_CardExample_Content.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Detected in conversation about time management. June 15, 2025.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_Managing_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Managing Insights</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_Managing1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Browse all insights on the Dashboard, the Past Self page, or via the Command Palette ("&gt;insight: …"). Sort by recency, by topic, or by your own ★ ratings. Filter by date range or Collection.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_Managing2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Click any card to jump back to the originating conversation with the insight passage highlighted in context. Right-click to copy with attribution, save as an Annotation, promote to a Knowledge Vault note, or schedule a follow-up reflection prompt.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_InsightHarvesting_Managing3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Star, dismiss, or annotate — dismissed insights stop appearing automatically and never surface again; starred insights are featured prominently in the Weekly Digest and the Dashboard. Your dismiss/keep signal trains the detector to be more selective with similar future patterns.</value></data>

  <!-- ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       USER GUIDE: GETTING STARTED TUTORIALS (NEW)
       ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <!-- UserGuide: First Chat Tutorial (Row 4) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstChat_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Your First Chat</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstChat_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>A 90-second hands-on tutorial that takes you from "fresh install" to "first useful conversation." By the end you will understand the chat surface, how to swap between local and cloud models, what conversation context means, and how to use the few keyboard shortcuts that make every subsequent chat 3× faster.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstChat_Step1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Step 1: Open AI Chat</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstChat_Step1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Click the AI Chat entry in the sidebar, press Ctrl+2 (the navigation order is Dashboard, Chat, AskFiles, Search, Vault…), or hit Ctrl+K and type "chat." The interface shows the conversation list on the left, the active conversation in the main pane, and the model selector + composer at the top and bottom respectively.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstChat_Step2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Step 2: Select a Model</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstChat_Step2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Use the model dropdown at the top of the conversation. For local-only inference, pick an Ollama model — llama3.2 is a strong default. For frontier capability with cloud opt-in, pick OpenAI GPT-4o (or GPT-4o-mini for speed/cost) or Anthropic Claude Sonnet 4 (best balance of reasoning and speed). The dropdown shows context-window length and per-token cost (for cloud models) so you can pick the right tool for the job.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstChat_Step3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Step 3: Start Chatting</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstChat_Step3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Type a message in the composer at the bottom. Enter sends; Shift+Enter inserts a newline; Ctrl+Enter sends and immediately starts a new turn. Responses stream token-by-token, render full Markdown live (including code blocks with syntax highlighting), and end with three suggested follow-up questions. Press Esc at any point to stop generation; the partial response is preserved.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstChat_Tips_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>First Chat Tips</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstChat_Tip1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Be specific. "Summarize this article" beats "summarize this." "Summarize this article in five bullets, focused on cost implications" beats both. Clear prompts produce dramatically better answers — especially on smaller local models.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstChat_Tip2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Press Ctrl+N to start a fresh conversation. Each conversation has its own context window, so a new conversation is the right move when you switch topics or want the model to forget prior framing. Pin important conversations to the top of the sidebar with the pin icon.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstChat_Tip3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Iterate with follow-ups instead of long opening prompts. Agent-X remembers everything within a conversation, so the second question can be very short ("now compare to last quarter") and still get the full context. This is faster than crafting one mega-prompt and produces better answers.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Your First Document (Row 5) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstDocument_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Your First Document</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstDocument_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Agent-X becomes substantially more useful the moment you connect it to your own documents. This tutorial walks you from "first import" to "first grounded answer with citations" in under three minutes. The same flow scales seamlessly from one file to thousands.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstDocument_Step1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Step 1: Import a File</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstDocument_Step1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Knowledge Vault (Ctrl+I), then drag a PDF, Word doc, Markdown, text, code, or web URL onto the window. Alternatively click Import to use the system file picker, paste a URL to ingest a web page, or drag a whole folder to recursively import everything inside it. The first import also kicks off auto-tagging and duplicate detection.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstDocument_Step2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Step 2: Wait for Indexing</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstDocument_Step2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Indexing extracts text via the format-specific parser, chunks the content into ~512-token semantic segments with overlap, runs each chunk through your embedding model, and writes vectors to the HNSW index plus FTS5 keyword index. Time scales with size: a typical PDF takes 5-30 seconds, a 1,000-page book around 2 minutes. Progress shows live in the Vault row and in Operations (Ctrl+Shift+O).</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstDocument_Step3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Step 3: Ask Questions</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstDocument_Step3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open AI Chat, scope the conversation to the freshly imported document (or skip scoping to query the whole vault), and ask. Try "What are the main points?", "Summarize the key arguments and counter-arguments," or "What evidence does the author provide for their central claim?" Every answer renders with numbered citations linking back to the exact passages used.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstDocument_Supported_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Supported File Types</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_FirstDocument_Supported.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Documents: PDF (with OCR fallback), DOCX, RTF, EPUB, HTML, Markdown, plain text, CSV/TSV, XLSX (sheet extraction), PPTX (slide extraction). Code: 30+ languages with syntax-aware chunking. Web: any URL via paste or the browser extension. Audio: MP3/M4A/WAV/FLAC via Audio Transcription. YouTube URLs trigger transcript ingestion. Images: PNG/JPG/JPEG via Tesseract OCR (and direct vision-model querying when the active model supports images).</value></data>

  <!-- ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       USER GUIDE: RESEARCH & WEB (NEW SECTIONS)
       ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <!-- UserGuide: Web Search (Row 13) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Web Search &amp; Research</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Agent-X integrates live web search to bring current information into your conversations. Configure one of three pluggable providers — Brave Search, Serper.dev, or a self-hosted SearXNG instance — and Agent-X will fetch fresh results, extract clean article content, and synthesize a cited answer alongside your local Knowledge Vault. Results are cached locally to cut latency and API spend on repeat queries.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_HowItWorks_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>How Web Search Works</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Step1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Configure a provider in Settings &gt; Web Search: Brave Search (paste your Brave API key), Serper.dev (Google-backed, paste your Serper API key), or SearXNG (paste the URL of your self-hosted instance — no key needed). Agent-X automatically falls back to the next configured provider if the preferred one fails or is rate-limited.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Step2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>When a query triggers web search, Agent-X issues the search, retrieves the top results, fetches each source page, and runs the HTML through a readability extractor to strip nav, ads, and chrome — leaving clean main-article text. Optionally, JavaScript-rendered pages are processed through Playwright via the JsRendering service.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Step3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Extracted content is fed to the chat model along with your question. The model synthesizes a grounded answer with numbered citations linking back to each source URL. Source pages can be one-click imported into your Knowledge Vault for future RAG queries.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Modes_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Search Modes</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Mode1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Automatic (Default)</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Mode1_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The model detects when a question needs current information (recent events, live data, fact-checks, "today/this week" framing) and triggers web search transparently. The composer shows a "Searching the web…" indicator while the search runs.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Mode2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Manual Search</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Mode2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Force a web search by prefixing your message with "/search " or by toggling the Web button in the composer. Useful when you want fresh sources even for evergreen topics, or to bypass the auto-detection heuristic for borderline queries.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Mode3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>RAG + Web Combined</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Mode3_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Agent-X retrieves from both your Knowledge Vault and live web in a single query. Citations are tagged with their provenance ("Vault" or "Web") so you can immediately see what came from your own materials versus the open internet. Ideal for research where you want to compare your prior work against the current state of the field.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Tips_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Web Search Tips</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Tip1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Ask for sources — Agent-X always returns inline numbered citations linking to source URLs. Click any citation to open the page in your default browser, or right-click to import the page into your Knowledge Vault for permanent indexing.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebSearch_Tip2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Request recency checks — Phrases like "as of today," "is this still accurate in 2026?", or "what changed in the last month?" automatically trigger fresh searches. The cache respects a configurable TTL so stale answers don't persist when the underlying topic evolves.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Web Content Import (Row 14) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Web Content Import</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Beyond local files, Agent-X imports any web content into your Knowledge Vault — articles, blog posts, documentation pages, RSS/Atom feeds, sitemaps, even YouTube transcripts. Imported pages get the same treatment as local files: text extraction, chunking, embedding, FTS5 indexing, auto-tagging, and full availability to RAG, Search, Quick Actions, and Workflows.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_Methods_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Import Methods</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_Method1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Paste URL directly — On the Web Import page (Ctrl+Shift+E) paste any URL, optionally tag it, and pick the destination Collection. Agent-X fetches, extracts, deduplicates against existing imports, and indexes — typically in 3-8 seconds for an article.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_Method2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Browser extension — Install the Agent-X Chrome/Edge extension and capture the page you're reading with a single click. The extension communicates with the desktop app over native messaging, so capture happens locally with no cloud round-trip.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_Method3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Batch &amp; feed-based import — Paste a list of URLs from a text file or spreadsheet, point Agent-X at an RSS/Atom feed, or import from a sitemap.xml URL. Feed-based imports can be set to refresh on a schedule via the FeedService, giving you a hands-off pipeline for monitoring blogs, news sources, and documentation portals.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_Extraction_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Content Extraction</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_Extraction_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Agent-X intelligently extracts the meaningful content from each page:</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_Extraction1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Strips navigation, sidebars, footers, ads, cookie banners, and chrome using a readability heuristic — only the main article body is kept. The supplementary helper preserves embedded image alt-text and figure captions where they add information.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_Extraction2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Preserves structure — heading hierarchy, lists, tables, blockquotes, code blocks, and inline links are all maintained so semantic chunking respects natural boundaries and citations resolve to specific sections.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_Extraction3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Captures rich metadata — author, publication date, canonical URL, og:image, language, and reading time. YouTube URLs trigger the YouTubeTranscriptHelper which pulls the auto-captions or published transcript as the document body.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_JsRendering_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>JavaScript Rendering</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_JsRendering_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>For SPA-style sites that load content dynamically (React/Vue/Svelte apps, paywalled sites with JS gates, infinite-scroll archives), enable JS rendering in Settings &gt; Web Import. Agent-X spins up a headless Playwright/Chromium instance, lets the page hydrate, optionally scrolls to trigger lazy loading, and captures the post-render DOM.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_JsRendering_Note_Label.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Note</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WebImport_JsRendering_Note.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>JS rendering is 5-15× slower than static fetch and uses ~200 MB of RAM per concurrent page. Enable it only for sites that need it. Per-domain overrides let you keep static fetch for fast sites and JS rendering for the few that require it.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Citations & Sources (Row 15) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_Citations_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Citations &amp; Sources</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Citations_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Transparency is non-negotiable in Agent-X. Every answer grounded in your documents or in live web sources ships with explicit, clickable citations — not hand-wavy "according to your files" gestures, but specific [1], [2], [3] markers tied to specific passages with verifiable provenance. A guard pass at render time validates that every citation number maps to a real retrieved chunk, so the model cannot invent citations even under hallucination pressure.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Citations_Types_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Citation Types</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Citations_Type1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Document Citations</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Citations_Type1_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>For Knowledge Vault sources: file name, page number (PDF), heading path (Markdown/Word), or chunk index, plus relevance score and the exact retrieved passage. Click to open the document at that location with the matched span highlighted; right-click to add the source to a Collection or pin it for the conversation.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Citations_Type2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Web Citations</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Citations_Type2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>For live web sources: page title, canonical URL, source domain favicon, publication date when available, and relevance score. Click to open in your default browser; right-click to import the page into the Knowledge Vault as a permanent record so future RAG queries can ground in it without another web fetch.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Citations_Type3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Multi-Source Answers</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Citations_Type3_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>When an answer fuses Knowledge Vault and Web sources, each citation is tagged with its provenance ("Vault" / "Web") and listed in a unified References panel grouped by source type. The model is instructed to attribute each claim to its specific source, so multi-source answers remain auditable rather than averaged into a single opaque blob.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Citations_Using_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Using Citations</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Citations_Using1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Click any citation chip to expand the source preview, jump to the original location, or copy the cited passage with attribution to your clipboard for use in your own writing.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Citations_Using2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Hover citation numbers inline in AI responses to surface a quick popup with the source title, score, and excerpt — verify a claim without breaking your reading flow.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Citations_Using3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>In the Knowledge Vault, sort or filter by citation frequency to see which documents your conversations rely on most. High-frequency sources are obvious candidates for promotion to a pinned Collection or for deeper annotation.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Browser Extension (Row 16) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_BrowserExtension_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Browser Extension</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BrowserExtension_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Agent-X browser extension is your in-browser bridge to the desktop app. Save the page you're reading to the Knowledge Vault in one click, ask grounded questions about the current tab without leaving the page, run Quick Actions on selected text, and pull pages or YouTube transcripts straight into Workflows. The extension talks to the desktop app over secure local Native Messaging — no cloud, no proxy, no third party.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BrowserExtension_Installation_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Installation</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BrowserExtension_Step1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Install the Agent-X extension from the Chrome Web Store or Microsoft Edge Add-ons (both Chromium-based browsers are fully supported). Firefox is on the roadmap. The extension is open-source and shipped from the same repository as the desktop app.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BrowserExtension_Step2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>On first launch, the extension prompts to register the Native Messaging host. Confirm — this is a one-time, per-user step that writes a registry entry pointing the browser at the local Agent-X messaging endpoint. Without this, the extension cannot reach the desktop app.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BrowserExtension_Step3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Pin the extension icon to your toolbar, then sign in to your local Agent-X session by clicking "Pair" — the desktop app generates a one-time code that you confirm in the extension. The pairing is durable and re-establishes automatically after browser or app restarts.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BrowserExtension_Features_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Extension Features</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BrowserExtension_Feature1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Quick save — Click the extension icon (or use the configurable hotkey) to capture the current page into your Knowledge Vault. The extension applies the same readability extractor as Web Import, so you get clean main-content not raw HTML, with metadata preserved.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BrowserExtension_Feature2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Page chat — Open the side panel to chat about the current page. The page content is loaded as ad-hoc context — including images and tables — without the page being permanently indexed, so you can quickly interrogate articles you don't want cluttering your vault.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BrowserExtension_Feature3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Selection analysis — Highlight any text on any page, right-click, and pick from "Explain," "Summarize," "Translate," "Save as Annotation," or "Send to Quick Actions." Selections can also be sent to a specific Workflow as input.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BrowserExtension_Feature4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Smart capture — Auto-detects article boundaries on most reader-friendly sites; falls back to a manual "select the area to capture" affordance for tricky layouts. YouTube tabs detect transcript availability and offer one-click transcript import.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BrowserExtension_NativeMessaging_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Native Messaging Architecture</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BrowserExtension_NativeMessaging_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Communication runs over Chrome's Native Messaging protocol — JSON over stdio between the extension and a small registered host process that forwards to the running Agent-X app. No HTTP, no localhost server, no exposed ports. If Agent-X is not running, the extension queues actions locally and replays them when the app comes back. Everything happens on your machine; nothing transits a third-party server.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Mobile Companion (Row 17) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Mobile Companion App</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Agent-X mobile companion (iOS and Android) is a thin client that talks to the desktop app's local API host. Your phone never holds a copy of your full vault — instead, it streams chat, search, and document access on demand from your desktop over an end-to-end encrypted channel anchored on your home network or a paired tunnel. The desktop remains the single source of truth; the phone is a window into it.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Pairing_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Pairing Your Device</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Step1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Install Agent-X Mobile from the App Store or Google Play. The mobile build is open-source and ships independently of the desktop release cycle, so you can run desktop 1.2 with mobile 1.4 (or vice-versa) without compatibility issues.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Step2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>On the desktop, open Settings &gt; Mobile Companion and click "Pair New Device." Agent-X starts the local API host (if not already running), generates a fresh keypair for the device, and renders a one-time QR code containing the local endpoint, the device's symmetric session key, and a short pairing code that expires in 5 minutes.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Step3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Scan the QR code with the mobile app and confirm the matching pairing code on the desktop. The mobile app stores the encrypted device key in the OS keychain (iOS Keychain / Android Keystore). All subsequent traffic is mutually authenticated and encrypted with that key — your data never traverses a Strategia server.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Features_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Mobile Features</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Feature1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Full Chat Access</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Feature1_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Continue conversations started on desktop, or start fresh ones from the phone. Streaming responses, citations, code blocks, and Markdown all render natively. New conversations sync back to the desktop app within seconds when both are online.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Feature2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Knowledge Search</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Feature2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Run hybrid Search across your entire Knowledge Vault from anywhere — meeting prep, fact-checking on the road, or pulling a quote into a message. Results, citations, and document previews stream from the desktop in real time.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Feature3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Quick Capture</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Feature3_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Voice notes (transcribed locally on the desktop via the Audio Transcription service) and text notes drop straight into your Knowledge Vault. Photo capture runs OCR before indexing — turn a page from a book or a whiteboard into a searchable document in seconds.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Feature4_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Offline Mode</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Feature4_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Recent conversations, pinned documents, and the last 50 search results are cached on-device and remain accessible without a connection. Captures made offline are queued and replay automatically when the desktop is reachable again.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Sync_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Data Synchronization &amp; Security</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_MobileCompanion_Sync_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Sync runs through your local desktop API host. On a shared LAN this is direct; remotely, you can pair via a Tailscale, Zerotier, or your own reverse proxy. The desktop is always the authority — the phone holds only what it needs for its current view, and revoking a paired device from Settings invalidates its key immediately and wipes its local cache on next launch.</value></data>

  <!-- ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       USER GUIDE: AUTOMATION (NEW SECTIONS)
       ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <!-- UserGuide: Workflows (Row 23) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Workflows &amp; Automation</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Workflows are multi-step AI pipelines you compose from five building blocks — AI prompts, document lookups, text transforms, conditional branches, and output formatting — and run on demand. Chain them to turn repetitive work into a single click: summarize a document set, extract key points and reformat them as a report, or drive any custom text pipeline you would otherwise execute by hand. Build and run them visually in the Workflow Builder (Ctrl+Shift+W).</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_Creating_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Creating Workflows</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_CreateStep1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open the Workflows panel from Settings or the Quick Actions menu. Click "Create New Workflow" to start.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_CreateStep2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Define a trigger — this could be a schedule (daily at 9 AM), an event (new document added), or manual execution.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_CreateStep3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Add actions — each workflow can include multiple actions like AI queries, document indexing, or notifications.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_CreateStep4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Test your workflow with a manual run, then activate it. Monitor execution logs to verify it works as expected.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_Triggers_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Running Workflows</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_Trigger1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>On-Demand Runs</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_Trigger1_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Run any workflow from the Workflows page or the Workflow Builder whenever you need it. Each run takes your input text, feeds it through the step chain, and presents the final output.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_Trigger2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Step Library</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_Trigger2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Five step types compose every workflow: AI Prompt (call a model with a templated prompt), Document Lookup (pull content from your vault), Text Transform (case, extraction, dedupe, and more), Conditional Branch (if-then logic on step output), and Output Format (Markdown, HTML, or JSON).</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_Trigger3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Run History</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_Trigger3_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Every run is recorded with per-step output, duration, and success status, so you can inspect exactly what each step produced and where a pipeline needs adjustment.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_Examples_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Example Workflows</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_Example1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>"Meeting Minutes" — paste a raw transcript, extract decisions and action items with an AI Prompt step, and format the result as a Markdown summary you can file or share.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_Example2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>"Research Digest" — look up documents from your Research collection with a Document Lookup step, have the model pull out key findings, and produce a structured briefing on demand.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Workflows_Example3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>"Content Repurposer" — condense a long document with an AI Prompt step, then use a Conditional Branch: short outputs become social posts, longer ones become a formatted article draft.</value></data>

