  <!-- UserGuide: Advanced Queries (Row 31) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_AdvancedQueries_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Advanced Queries</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AdvancedQueries_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Past simple questions, Agent-X supports a deep set of query techniques that materially improve precision, recall, and answer quality. The more deliberate your phrasing, the better your results — these techniques are the difference between "useful" answers and "remarkably good" ones, especially when your vault grows past a few hundred documents.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AdvancedQueries_Techniques_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Query Techniques</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AdvancedQueries_Technique1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Collection &amp; tag scoping — Constrain retrieval with natural-language scope hints ("in the Competitors collection," "tagged 'pricing'") or use the scope chips above the composer. Combining a Collection with a tag commonly improves answer precision by 20-40% by eliminating off-topic chunks before retrieval. Hybrid retrieval also accepts boolean hints: "must contain 'ARR'" forces a keyword match alongside the semantic search.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AdvancedQueries_Technique2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Time-bounding &amp; recency — Phrase queries with explicit windows ("from the past week," "Q3 only," "after the Series B announcement"). Agent-X applies date filters to source metadata when present, and prompts the model to weight recent passages over older ones for evolving topics. Pair with a Collection filter for "what changed in the Pricing collection between Q2 and Q4."</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AdvancedQueries_Technique3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Comparative queries — Ask for explicit comparisons across documents or sources ("How do the three proposals differ on timeline?", "Compare our roadmap to what competitors announced this quarter"). Agent-X retrieves the top-K passages per source/document independently, then prompts the model to synthesize a comparative answer rather than blending sources into a single voice. Comparison view in the dedicated Comparison page surfaces side-by-side diffs.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AdvancedQueries_Technique4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Structured output — Request specific output shapes ("answer as a Markdown table with columns Feature / Price / Pros / Cons," "give me a bullet list grouped by quarter," "respond with a JSON object matching this schema"). Cloud models with native tool-use (OpenAI, Anthropic) honor JSON-schema requests with strict mode — useful for piping AI output into Workflows or external systems.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Performance Tuning (Row 32) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_PerformanceTuning_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Performance Tuning</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PerformanceTuning_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Agent-X is built to perform well on a wide range of hardware, from a 4 GB-VRAM laptop to a workstation with multiple GPUs. The defaults target the middle of that spectrum (16 GB RAM, 8 GB VRAM, 7B-class chat model). Tuning the knobs below to your machine and workload can deliver 2-5× speedups, halve memory usage, and meaningfully improve answer quality. Run the Hardware Advisor first — it sets sensible starting values for your specific configuration.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PerformanceTuning_Memory_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Memory Management</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PerformanceTuning_Memory.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Vector embeddings and the HNSW index live in RAM for fast retrieval. The "Max in-memory chunks" setting (Settings &gt; Knowledge Vault) caps how many chunks stay hot — reduce it on 8 GB systems (try 50,000), keep the default 250,000 on 16 GB, push to 1M+ on 32 GB. SQLCipher encryption costs ~10-15% throughput; the Encryption setting in Settings &gt; Sync lets you trade encryption for speed in trusted environments. For very large vaults, enable on-disk HNSW with mmap — slightly slower retrieval but bounded memory.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PerformanceTuning_Queries_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Query Optimization</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PerformanceTuning_Queries.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Tune retrieval top-K (default 10): 5-7 is faster and often plenty for narrow queries; 15-20 helps recall on broad questions but increases prompt tokens and latency. Set MMR diversification lambda to 0.3-0.5 to deduplicate near-identical chunks. Cache hit rate and average retrieval latency are surfaced in the Operations page — if cache hit rate is below 30%, your queries are scattered enough that the cache is not earning its cost; consider disabling SearchCache or increasing its TTL.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PerformanceTuning_Local_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Local Model Tuning</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_PerformanceTuning_Local.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>For Ollama: smaller quantization (Q4_K_M) uses ~50% less VRAM than Q8_0 with a typically 2-5% quality drop — usually the right trade. Cap context length to 4K for Q&amp;A workloads, leave it at 8K-16K for long-document RAG. Maximize GPU layer offload (num_gpu) until you start spilling to CPU; the sweet spot is one or two layers below the spill point. For embeddings, all-minilm is 10× faster than mxbai-embed-large with comparable retrieval quality on most workloads — use it unless you have a specific reason to prefer the larger model. Throughput is logged in Operations; chase tokens-per-second, not theoretical model rankings.</value></data>

