  <!-- UserGuide: Annotations & Highlights -->
  <data name="UserGuide_Annotations_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Annotations &amp; Highlights</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Annotations_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Annotations turn the Knowledge Vault into a place to think, not just store. Highlight passages while reading, attach notes and tags, link annotations across documents, and surface them as first-class search results, RAG context, and digest material. Every annotation is tied to its exact source span — color, comment, and cross-references survive document re-indexing and exports.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Annotations_Creating_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Creating Annotations</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Annotations_Create1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Highlight while reading — Open any document in the Vault preview pane, select text, and pick a color (yellow / green / blue / pink / purple). Each color can be assigned a meaning in Settings (e.g., yellow = "key claim," pink = "to verify," green = "agree").</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Annotations_Create2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Add notes &amp; tags — Attach freeform notes to any highlight; add tags to make annotations searchable across documents. Notes support Markdown including embedded links to other documents, conversations, or other annotations.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Annotations_Create3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Capture from anywhere — Save selections from AI Chat citations, Web Search results, the browser extension, and Quick Action outputs as annotations on the source document with one click.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Annotations_Surfacing_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Where Annotations Surface</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Annotations_Surface1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Annotations page — A dedicated workspace listing every highlight and note across the entire Vault, filterable by document, color, tag, date, and free-text search. Bulk-export to Markdown for review or migration.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Annotations_Surface2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Inline in source documents — When you reopen a document, all your annotations are visible in-line; clicking the gutter mark opens the linked note. The same view exports to PDF for sharing annotated documents externally.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Annotations_Surface3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>RAG context boost — When asking grounded questions, annotated passages are weighted higher in retrieval. The model is told "the user has marked these passages as important" — you can see the difference in answer quality on heavily annotated documents.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Annotations_Surface4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Weekly Digest &amp; Insights — Annotated passages and your notes flow into the Weekly Digest as a "what you marked this week" section, and contribute to insight detection and Knowledge Graph edge weighting.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Audio Transcription -->
  <data name="UserGuide_AudioTranscription_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Audio Transcription</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AudioTranscription_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Drop audio files (MP3, M4A, WAV, FLAC, OGG, WebM) or recordings from the mobile app into Agent-X and they're transcribed locally with Whisper-class models, indexed into the Knowledge Vault with timestamped chunks, and available to every other feature — RAG, Search, Quick Actions, Workflows. Voice memos, podcast episodes, meeting recordings, lectures, interviews — all become first-class searchable content.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AudioTranscription_HowItWorks_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>How Transcription Works</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AudioTranscription_Step1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Import via drag-and-drop, file picker, the mobile companion's voice-memo capture, or a folder watch. Files queue in the Audio Transcription pipeline and the indexing queue shows live progress in Operations.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AudioTranscription_Step2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Local Whisper-class transcription runs on your machine — pick a model variant in Settings (tiny/base/small/medium/large) trading speed for accuracy. CPU-only is supported; GPU dramatically accelerates large models. Language is auto-detected (100+ languages supported); you can pin it for known sources.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AudioTranscription_Step3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Output is a fully timestamped transcript chunked at speaker-aware boundaries (when the model supports diarization). Chunks embed into the Vault like any other document, but with the added benefit that citations resolve to specific timestamps you can click to jump to in the audio playback panel.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AudioTranscription_UseCases_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Use Cases</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AudioTranscription_UseCase1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Meeting recall — Drop a meeting recording in, then ask "what did we decide about Q4 hiring?" and get a timestamp-linked answer with the exact quote and click-to-play.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AudioTranscription_UseCase2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Podcast research — Build a Collection of podcast episodes; query across them like a regular text vault. "Which episodes discuss X?" with citations you can listen to.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AudioTranscription_UseCase3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Voice journaling — Capture voice notes on the mobile app; they auto-transcribe and feed into Temporal Identity tracking just like written messages do.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AudioTranscription_Note_Label.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Note</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_AudioTranscription_Note.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Audio Transcription is gated by the experimental.audio_transcription feature flag and may require an additional model download. Enable it in Settings &gt; Feature Flags. Smaller models run real-time on a 4-core CPU; large models benefit from a GPU.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Workspace Profiles -->
  <data name="UserGuide_WorkspaceProfiles_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Workspace Profiles</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WorkspaceProfiles_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Workspace Profiles let you switch entire Agent-X contexts in one click — different document scopes, default models, system prompts, color themes, and even feature-flag sets per profile. Have a "Work" profile that sees only your work Vault and uses Claude Sonnet 4 by default, and a "Personal" profile that sees journal entries and uses a local Ollama model. Profiles isolate cleanly without sandboxing — the same vault can serve multiple profiles by Collection scope.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WorkspaceProfiles_Creating_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Creating Profiles</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WorkspaceProfiles_Create1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Workspace Profile (sidebar, or Command Palette → "workspace") and click New Profile. Pick a name, color tag, and (optionally) a Collection scope that constrains every retrieval and Search to those Collections only.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WorkspaceProfiles_Create2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Configure profile-specific defaults: chat model, embedding model, system prompt, theme (light/dark/auto), enabled feature flags, default RAG top-K, and which sidebar surfaces are visible. Anything not set inherits from your global Settings.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WorkspaceProfiles_Create3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Switch profiles via the profile dropdown in the title bar or Ctrl+Shift+S → pick from the list. Switching is instant; no app restart, no re-index, no data migration. Conversations and annotations remain bound to the profile they were created in.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WorkspaceProfiles_UseCases_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Use Cases</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WorkspaceProfiles_UseCase1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Work / Personal separation — Two profiles, two scopes, two model defaults. Avoid leaking work-context model habits into personal exploration and vice-versa, all without spinning up a separate install.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WorkspaceProfiles_UseCase2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Client isolation — Consultants and contractors create per-client profiles so each client's docs, conversations, and notes are cleanly partitioned. One-click switch between Acme Corp and BetaCo with zero context bleed.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_WorkspaceProfiles_UseCase3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Project workspaces — Spin up a profile for a heads-down sprint with a custom system prompt ("you are a research assistant for X project"), pinned Collections, and a different theme so it visually feels like a separate workspace.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Plugins -->
  <data name="UserGuide_Plugins_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Plugins &amp; Extensibility</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Plugins_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Plugins extend Agent-X with new providers, parsers, Quick Actions, Workflow nodes, sidebar surfaces, and integrations. They're packaged as .agentx-plugin files (or .zip), shipped through the Plugin Manager (Settings &gt; Plugins), and run sandboxed inside the desktop app. Built-in plugins include Calendar (Outlook/Google) and Email; the developer SDK lets you build your own — anything from a custom RAG pipeline to a "send to Notion" action.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Plugins_Manager_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Plugin Manager</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Plugins_Manager1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Install — Drag a .agentx-plugin or .zip onto the Plugin Manager, or browse the catalog and click Install. Plugins declare permissions on install (network, file-system, model access); review and approve before activation.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Plugins_Manager2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Configure — Each plugin exposes its own settings panel (API keys, sync intervals, scope filters). Configuration is stored encrypted alongside Agent-X settings and survives upgrades. Plugin-contributed commands appear automatically in the Command Palette.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Plugins_Manager3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Enable / disable / uninstall — Toggle plugins on and off without uninstalling. Disabled plugins keep their config but stop running. Uninstall removes all plugin files and configuration; uninstallation is final and confirmed via the Danger Zone.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Plugins_Capabilities_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>What Plugins Can Do</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Plugins_Capability1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Custom AI providers — Wire in any OpenAI-compatible or Anthropic-compatible API endpoint (self-hosted Mistral, Together, Groq, OpenRouter, Replicate, Fireworks, etc.). Provider plugins integrate with Cost Tracking, Settings, and the Model Manager.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Plugins_Capability2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Custom parsers &amp; sources — Add support for new file formats, niche sources (Notion exports, Logseq journals, Roam graphs), or specialized indexers. Plugin parsers participate in the indexing queue like built-in parsers.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Plugins_Capability3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Workflow nodes &amp; Quick Actions — Contribute new action nodes (send to Slack, post to webhook, append to spreadsheet, run a script) or new Quick Action templates. Plugins extend the same builder UI users already know.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Plugins_Capability4.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Sidebar pages &amp; integrations — Plugins can add new pages with their own UI, integrate with Calendar/Email/Inbox, or contribute to the Knowledge Graph. The plugin SDK uses the same WinUI 3 components as the rest of the app for visual consistency.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Calendar & Email Integrations -->
  <data name="UserGuide_Integrations_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Calendar &amp; Email Integrations</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Integrations_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Connect Microsoft Outlook, Google Workspace, or any standard CalDAV / IMAP / Exchange Web Services source and Agent-X starts treating your calendar events and emails as first-class knowledge — searchable, queryable, and summarizable alongside your documents. The integration is read-only by default; opt-in send and respond capabilities require explicit additional permission grants.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Integrations_Calendar_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Calendar Integration</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Integrations_Calendar1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Connect Outlook (OAuth via Microsoft Graph), Google Calendar (OAuth via Google), or any CalDAV provider. The CalendarSyncService pulls events on a schedule (default every 15 minutes), respects per-calendar visibility filters, and indexes events as searchable items.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Integrations_Calendar2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Ask natural-language questions across your calendar — "What's on my plate next week?", "Who am I meeting from Acme?", "Find every recurring meeting longer than 60 minutes," "Summarize Q3 meeting load by participant." Answers cite specific events with click-through to the source app.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Integrations_Calendar3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Use calendar events as Workflow triggers — auto-prep a meeting brief 15 minutes before each event by querying related Knowledge Vault docs and emails about the attendees, post the brief to your inbox or a Slack channel, and capture meeting outcomes back to the Vault.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Integrations_Email_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Email Integration</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Integrations_Email1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Connect via OAuth (Outlook, Gmail) or IMAP (any provider). Configure folder filters (e.g., index Inbox + Sent, skip Promotions and Updates) and retention windows. Indexed emails participate in RAG and Search like any other document.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Integrations_Email2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Query your email history — "Find every email about the Smith contract," "Summarize my conversation with Jane this quarter," "What deadlines did anyone send me this month?" Email threads are coalesced so a 12-message thread retrieves as one coherent unit, not 12 fragments.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Integrations_Email3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Email-driven automation — Trigger Workflows on incoming email matching a filter (sender, subject, label), use AI to draft responses in your voice via Generative Identity, queue them for one-click send, or route incoming attachments straight into the Knowledge Vault.</value></data>

  <!-- UserGuide: Backup & Restore -->
  <data name="UserGuide_BackupRestore_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Backup &amp; Restore</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BackupRestore_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Backup &amp; Restore is the round-trip story for your data: complete, encrypted, schedulable backups that can rebuild your entire Agent-X state on any machine. Backups capture the SQLite database, the document store, plugin data, and settings — everything you need to walk away from this device and pick up exactly where you left off on another. Restores support full replace, smart merge, and cherry-pick.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BackupRestore_Backup_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Creating Backups</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BackupRestore_Backup1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Manual backup — Settings &gt; Backup &gt; Create Backup Now produces a single .agentxbackup file (a compressed encrypted archive). Pick a destination, optionally set a passphrase, and you're done. Typical backup size is roughly the size of your imported documents plus 10-15% for vectors and metadata.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BackupRestore_Backup2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Scheduled backups — Configure cadence (daily, weekly), destination (local folder, network share, or any synced cloud-drive folder like OneDrive/Dropbox/Google Drive), retention policy (keep last N, last M days), and passphrase. The BackupService runs in the background; failures surface a notification with diagnostic details.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BackupRestore_Backup3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>What's included — full SQLite database (conversations, insights, beliefs, voice profile, annotations, search history, settings), the document store, plugin data and configuration, and Workspace Profiles. Cloud-provider API keys are encrypted with DPAPI by default and only roundtrip when the same Windows account opens the backup; pair-with-passphrase mode lets you cross-account restore.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BackupRestore_Restore_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Restoring from a Backup</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BackupRestore_Restore1.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Replace — Wipe current state and load the backup verbatim. The right choice for migrating to a new machine, recovering from corruption, or rolling back a bad change. The current state is auto-backed-up before replace, so the operation is reversible.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BackupRestore_Restore2.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Merge — Combine the backup with your current state. Conflicts (same conversation ID, same document hash) resolve by last-write-wins by default; configurable to prefer-backup or prefer-current. Useful when consolidating two Agent-X installs or recovering specific data without losing recent work.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BackupRestore_Restore3.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Cherry-Pick — A checklist UI that lets you preview the backup contents and selectively restore: pick specific conversations, document Collections, plugin configs, or settings to import. Optimal for partial recoveries and surgical migrations.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BackupRestore_Migration_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Cross-Version Migration</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_BackupRestore_Migration_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Backups carry a schema version. When you restore on a newer Agent-X, the migration pipeline upgrades the schema in place — adding new columns, re-indexing if embedding-model defaults changed, etc. You can restore a backup made years ago on the latest version without manual steps.</value></data>


  <!-- UserGuide: Export & Data Portability (Row 34) -->
  <data name="UserGuide_Export_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Export &amp; Data Portability</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Export_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Your data is exportable in open formats at every level — single conversations, full conversation history, individual documents, the entire Knowledge Vault, your voice profile, your insights, your beliefs, your settings, your plugin manifests. There is no lock-in: if you ever leave Agent-X, you walk out with everything in formats you can read and load anywhere. Backup &amp; Restore (covered in its own section) is the round-trip story; this section is about one-way export.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Export_Conversations_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Conversation Export</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Export_Conversations.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Export a single conversation, multi-select conversations, all conversations, or a Past Self time-window. Formats: Markdown (best for reading and sharing), JSON (full structured fidelity including system prompts, tool calls, branches, and citations), plain text (lossy but universal), and HTML (with rendered code highlighting and citations). Customize via Export Templates (Settings &gt; Export) — control what fields, anonymize names, redact secrets, and tune the layout.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Export_Documents_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Document &amp; Vault Export</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Export_Documents.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Export the entire Knowledge Vault as a ZIP containing the original files, the extracted text per document, the chunk index, and the vector embeddings (in a portable .vec format). The bundle is bit-for-bit reloadable on another Agent-X install — perfect for moving between machines, archiving a project, or handing a vault to a collaborator. You can also export a subset (single document, single Collection, date range) when you don't need the whole thing.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Export_Backup_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Automated Backups</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Export_Backup.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Schedule automatic backups in Settings &gt; Backup. Backups capture the SQLite database (conversations, insights, beliefs, voice profile, settings), the document store, and plugin data. Encrypt with a passphrase for safe cloud storage; compression is on by default. Set retention policy (keep last N, last M days), pick a destination (local folder, network share, OneDrive/Dropbox/Google Drive folder), and choose cadence (daily, weekly).</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Export_Restore_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Restoring Data</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Export_Restore.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Import any previous backup from Settings &gt; Backup &gt; Restore, or from a fresh install during onboarding. Restore modes: Replace (wipe current state and load the backup), Merge (combine with current state, last-write-wins on conflicts), or Cherry-Pick (a checklist of conversations, documents, and settings to bring over). Cross-version migration runs automatically — open a backup from any prior Agent-X version and the migration pipeline upgrades the schema in place.</value></data>


  <!-- Guide accuracy pass 2026-07: Comparison / Smart Inbox / Analytics / Operations / Collaborative Sync sections -->
  <data name="UserGuide_Comparison_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Document Comparison</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Comparison_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Comparison page generates a structured, AI-written comparative analysis of two or more documents from your Knowledge Vault. Select the documents, optionally set a focus topic to steer the analysis, and Agent-X retrieves each document's most relevant passages and synthesizes a side-by-side report.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Comparison_Feature1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Structured Analysis</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Comparison_Feature1_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Reports break down into shared themes, key differences, and the unique points each document contributes — with an overall summary up top.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Comparison_Feature2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Focus Topics</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Comparison_Feature2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Add an optional focus topic ("pricing terms", "methodology") and the comparison concentrates on that dimension instead of a general overview.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Comparison_Feature3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Markdown Export</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Comparison_Feature3_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Export any comparison report to Markdown for sharing, archiving, or pasting into your own documents.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Inbox_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Smart Inbox</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Inbox_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Smart Inbox is the triage queue for content entering your knowledge base. Captures from the browser extension, the mobile companion, and plugins land here first, so you decide what is worth keeping — instead of letting everything pile into the vault unsorted.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Inbox_Feature1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>AI Previews</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Inbox_Feature1_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Agent-X drafts a preview for each item — a suggested summary, target collection, and tags — so most triage decisions take one glance and one click.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Inbox_Feature2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Accept, Defer, or Reject</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Inbox_Feature2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Accept an item to import it into the vault under your chosen collection, defer it to decide later, or reject it to discard. Batch actions clear a full queue in seconds.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Inbox_Feature3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Capture Sources</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Inbox_Feature3_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Web captures from the browser extension, items sent from the mobile companion, and content contributed by plugins all flow into one queue with a consistent triage workflow.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Analytics_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Analytics</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Analytics_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Analytics page shows how your knowledge base and AI usage evolve: usage statistics, performance metrics, and activity trends across conversations, documents, and tokens — all computed locally from your own data.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Analytics_Feature1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Usage Overview</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Analytics_Feature1_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Headline counters for conversations, messages, documents, and token consumption keep the size and activity of your workspace visible at all times.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Analytics_Feature2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Activity Trends</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Analytics_Feature2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Daily charts of conversations and document imports reveal your working patterns and how your knowledge base grows over time.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Analytics_Feature3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Indexing Health</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Analytics_Feature3_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>A live indexing-completion indicator shows how much of your vault is fully embedded and searchable.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Operations_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Operations</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Operations_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Operations page (Ctrl+Shift+O) is the health console for everything running behind the scenes: connector status, collaborative sync state, indexing progress, and recent background activity — in one live snapshot.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Operations_Feature1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Status Snapshot</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Operations_Feature1_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>See at a glance whether calendar and email connectors, collaborative sync, and indexing are healthy, idle, or need attention.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Operations_Feature2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Suggested Fixes</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Operations_Feature2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>When something is off, Operations lists the highest-leverage remediation steps based on the current snapshot — connect a live source, enable sync, or finish configuration.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Operations_Feature3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Live Monitoring</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Operations_Feature3_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The snapshot refreshes as operations progress, so long-running work like bulk imports and re-indexing can be watched without leaving the page.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Sync_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Collaborative Sync</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Sync_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Collaborative Sync keeps multiple Agent-X installations in step using an encrypted, file-based transport: point every device at a shared folder — OneDrive, Google Drive, a NAS, or even a USB drive — and changes flow between them with no server and no cloud account required.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Sync_Feature1_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Shared-Folder Transport</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Sync_Feature1_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Any folder every machine can reach works as the sync channel. Agent-X writes encrypted change packages to it — the shared location never holds readable data.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Sync_Feature2_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Conflict Resolution</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Sync_Feature2_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Timestamp-based conflict resolution decides which change wins when the same item was edited on two devices — no manual merge steps required.</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Sync_Feature3_Title.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Local Collaboration Sessions</value></data>
  <data name="UserGuide_Sync_Feature3_Description.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>On a trusted local network, Agent-X installations can also host live collaboration sessions — sharing presence and activity events directly between peers, with nothing leaving your network.</value></data>

